{
  "id": "stand-by-me-to-basket-case",
  "title": "Stand by Me to Basket Case",
  "cues": [
    {
      "id": "intro",
      "anchor": {
        "segment": 0,
        "offset": 0.3
      },
      "kind": "intro",
      "text": "Ben E. King drew Stand by Me from a hymn by Charles Albert Tindley and a Soul Stirrers spiritual. In Mike Stoller's account, King sang the melody and Stoller built the bass line. It runs the fifties changes: one, six minor, four, five.",
      "image": "artist-Q356140",
      "sources": [
        {
          "title": "Stand by Me (Ben E. King song) (Wikipedia)",
          "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stand_by_Me_(Ben_E._King_song)"
        },
        {
          "title": "Ben E. King, Stand by Me (Hooktheory TheoryTab, chords)",
          "url": "https://www.hooktheory.com/theorytab/view/ben-e-king/stand-by-me"
        },
        {
          "title": "'50s progression (Wikipedia)",
          "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/%2750s_progression"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "band-aid-arrives",
      "anchor": {
        "segment": 1,
        "offset": 2.1
      },
      "kind": "changeover",
      "text": "Band Aid's Do They Know It's Christmas now sings over King's band. Midge Ure wrote its tune on a portable keyboard.",
      "image": null,
      "sources": [
        {
          "title": "Do They Know It's Christmas? (Wikipedia)",
          "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Do_They_Know_It%27s_Christmas%3F"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "band-aid",
      "anchor": {
        "segment": 1,
        "offset": 12.0
      },
      "kind": "song",
      "text": "Bob Geldof wrote the words after Michael Buerk's BBC reports on the famine in Ethiopia. He joked that Ure's tune sounded like the Z-Cars theme.",
      "image": null,
      "sources": [
        {
          "title": "Do They Know It's Christmas? (Wikipedia)",
          "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Do_They_Know_It%27s_Christmas%3F"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "green-day-arrives",
      "anchor": {
        "segment": 4,
        "offset": 2.1
      },
      "kind": "changeover",
      "text": "Green Day's Basket Case now rides the Band Aid band.",
      "image": "artist-Q47871",
      "sources": [
        {
          "title": "Basket Case (Green Day song) (Wikipedia)",
          "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Basket_Case_(Green_Day_song)"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "outro",
      "anchor": {
        "segment": 5,
        "offset": 1.0
      },
      "kind": "outro",
      "text": "Billie Joe Armstrong rewrote it about his panic attacks. Its verse closely mirrors Pachelbel's Canon. The graph links all three by one, four and five.",
